[{"id":"VRCE-7-63-1","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":1,"english":"There upon on the completion of the twelfth year, Satrughna, with few servants, soldiers and followers, desired to proceed to Ayodhyā protected by Rama.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-71-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063001","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"35f85ef3521519a24a61d2efa514cdb15606dcdabca752e13a5d0ab4c5ba33ec","compact":"322ac14f3bb14b7666d1d02a12a59e06b44e4d0137b6f64798a800edd0752bb9","nasal":"3ff2e676298e7a132770f162f243daa265140682510064122e61d3433860ee06"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-2","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":2,"english":"Hearing those words, Rāma sent for his councillors. The ministers of Dasaratha and Rāma, Brhaspati, Yayanta, Vijaya, Siddhartha, Rast ravarddhana, Asoka, Daramapāla the highly powerful Sumantra and others were all versed in laws, high-minded, conversant with all the Sastras, intelligent, born in respectable families, and adepts in counsel. Having invited them all and ascending his Puspaka car, he went to the place in question, and, descending there, asked the vulture, “How long has this nest been made? Tell me, if you remember it.\" Whereto the vulture replied:\"From the time when mankind were first born and spread over the four quarters of the globe have I been living in this nest.\"","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.47,"method":"fuzzy-nearby-witness","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-59-77"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063002","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d6a9f508e218f2b631060424af5c6206328fd7c765288b2df53e0fe15636595b","compact":"7f76ed9705f2ddbc1c3679d2158436735b37fc1af85f90314cf6ef915e119aa5","nasal":"59c1ea3e5028efb10562defe5ed93a1d15e6c9be16d797f4c4c03b25efd4a131"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-3","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":3,"english":"And having counted seven halting stations on his way, the highly illustrious descendant of Raghu, Satrughna, arrived at the hermitage of Valmiki.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.469,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-71-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063003","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"71076dd8074e4f1e82fa2467dd814fd4d100d38aa6ccd628c74d8968f35487f7","compact":"820275f8f66e188fc62d9fe9beb1c4d1ccc05542e7d556ab0bd934d5de137c60","nasal":"63d37eeaea627a318fef1f8f79bf192a29f39b1e6cc793eae2107e91e1c0cfa1"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-4","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":4,"english":"There upon having entered the highly picturesque city Ayodhyā, city Ayodhyā, the graceful descendant of Ikşvākus, ſatrughna, reached where the long-armed and the highly effulgent Råma was waiting.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.8,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-72-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063004","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5d7099d49fdbcbd5488204f605dbd3769291e3c8d3c3a8928dccdac803bc203e","compact":"19e07ff85ef8a03c4599266d085d2fe094b66e41faaeefc11172db9de7a11981","nasal":"658d8722e23221ded7c98e9ce242a26ec9a09f37535681ca70f66dc6e963d231"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-5","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":5,"english":"He espied there Rāma in the midst of the councillors like Indra among the immortals burning in his own effulgence. And having bowed with folded hands to Răma, having truth for his prowess, Śatrughna, said.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-72-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063005","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e10b8e44f81c9bb82bed6ec678eb598b925057fdb8a6471407a90e576736ea33","compact":"42e1bbb75ea78fcb66b6dbfbad600f8f4c21ecf7ca68eefa917e58542cdf3ebb","nasal":"6635d327eb81598c0c31f7d690044089bc9dcd841339f2ca1cf64bfd947476fa"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-6","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":6,"english":"O great king, I have carried out all your behests; I have slain the vicious Lavaņa and filled his city with subjects. O descendant of Raghu, twelve years have passed away without you, I do not wish to live any longer separated from you.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.66,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-72-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063006","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"85a5e08c4046253254f7b4a29c679412eff5b20cb25eb794f17b18e29bee54e4","compact":"0c18decc1e9ce614536e15fa2edd3ef291d726b04d79c2437d2fc7cb20f42cc7","nasal":"0c18decc1e9ce614536e15fa2edd3ef291d726b04d79c2437d2fc7cb20f42cc7"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-7","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":7,"english":"O great king, I have carried out all your behests; I have slain the vicious Lavaņa and filled his city with subjects. O descendant of Raghu, twelve years have passed away without you, I do not wish to live any longer separated from you.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.724,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-72-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063007","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"048e791378f1f72f87fef54c98256609114e25190fbd6289ce400a14ca98c970","compact":"f19acc7f1c02c962af9cc5af15de79c6ff1cecc9f6ed0ef481bbf1361329b880","nasal":"5a21a71788e195c63a4229b872806207dff8d63299359482b30be94fbc98bebd"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-8","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":8,"english":"Do you therefore, O Kākutstha of unmitigated prowess, have compassion on me. Without you, I cannot live for ever in a foreign province like a motherless child.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.731,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-72-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063008","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0de08a26158dd8994d8b086ed3ea49141663c874271bf473c5d9c54c55d712cd","compact":"c2473818f574ee7761af938b3885566ae4cbac20b5f5ece011107ff593496f53","nasal":"c2473818f574ee7761af938b3885566ae4cbac20b5f5ece011107ff593496f53"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-9","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":9,"english":"He having said this, Rāma embraced him and said: Be not sorry O hero. This is not a becoming action for Kşatriyas.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.638,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-72-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063009","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"29687d99d357c451734d47af0d94c2022eae825d6e67a8c8a5f89ff02de336af","compact":"88dcf10556f523db86079c6647277b64220ddd1a467f36f04c5d6a7726f4df66","nasal":"88dcf10556f523db86079c6647277b64220ddd1a467f36f04c5d6a7726f4df66"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-10","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":10,"english":"The kings, O Raghava, are never tired of living in a foreign land. According to the morality of the Kșatriyas, their greatest duty consists in governing the subjects.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.831,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-72-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063010","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"573d110733038c169a635d310c8efc474dca59515dcddfcf099631d2153df757","compact":"9db4bae9c0aa5918cc8ee5691b885d057e2fe19aab413a6e8183fa286b23b893","nasal":"b9e366f1756ade04a8b0426d0babadb0a1258ba80cbb7145142b3aa36995a89d"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-11","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":11,"english":"O heroic and foremost of men, do you, at intervals, come to Ayodhyå to see me and return to your own city.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.86,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-72-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063011","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"561e824441f02cd3a038845b01a56d8c86d5d48c3ed09c392be60c9d75fbdeca","compact":"81243c66c43ca3bf3a2b91fe05571622b854d7b41fd670929cfba29738b574b3","nasal":"f0b90f887858c5e019f96ab99e7dfdad9178b79a41f22aecda4d31500cd5b21e"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-12","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":12,"english":"Forsooth you are dearer than my life. But it is an incumbent duty to govern the kingdom. Do you therefore, O Kakutstha, live with me for seven nights, and afterwards return to your city with your servants, army and conveyances.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-72-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063012","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3a9ac81c577a075e2fe22eb80765b9d893082ff43ef6321c3f4168a1eff20f89","compact":"98e6a6ed71e19cbc9780c1866a13a1fd1e9ef764698ca9b468f0c8fc17690e9d","nasal":"98e6a6ed71e19cbc9780c1866a13a1fd1e9ef764698ca9b468f0c8fc17690e9d"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-63-13","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":63,"verse":13,"english":"Forsooth you are dearer than my life. But it is an incumbent duty to govern the kingdom. Do you therefore, O Kakutstha, live with me for seven nights, and afterwards return to your city with your servants, army and conveyances.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.651,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-72-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7063013","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","engli